# SealedSecret manifests. `spec.encryptedData` values are CIPHERTEXT, encrypted
# against this cluster's published public cert; only the in-cluster controller
# holds the private key. Being safe to commit is the entire point of Sealed
# Secrets — see docs/SECRETS_MANAGEMENT.md, which makes committing them the
# required workflow.
#
# generic.secrets.security.detected-generic-secret fires on the base64 blobs
# regardless, and re-fires on the whole file the moment a diff touches ANY key,
# so every routine credential rotation (including the self-service
# rotate-sealed-secret.yml workflow) would turn this check red for a finding
# that is a false positive by construction. A check that is red on every
# rotation is a check people learn to ignore.
#
# SCOPE NOTE: this directory holds ONLY SealedSecrets and their `.template`
# siblings. The trade-off is that a *plaintext* v1/Secret accidentally committed
# HERE would no longer be caught by this rule — so keep the directory to sealed
# manifests only. Detection is unchanged everywhere else in the repo, which is
# where a real leak would actually land.
deploy/sealed-secrets/

# fuzeinfra-keepalived image. keepalived manages network interfaces via netlink
# (adds/removes the VIP, sends VRRP) and its DaemonSet runs privileged with
# NET_ADMIN, so it MUST run as root — dockerfile.security.missing-user cannot be
# satisfied without breaking it (unlike the app Dockerfiles, which correctly use a
# non-root USER). Inline `nosemgrep` is not honored by this Semgrep setup, so the
# suppression is path-scoped here. The file is a 15-line apk-install; no other
# rule surface is meaningfully lost. See docs/runbooks/api-floating-vip.md.
images/keepalived/Dockerfile
